    {
      "id": "fd7dbab4-871a-47dd-a379-548731e0391c",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Discussion\n\nIn the StatsBomb subset, **57% of team-matches saw a side trail at some\npoint** — and **10.5% of those ended in a comeback win** (443 wins).\n\nThe shape of comebacks is brutally one-dimensional:\n\n- **Deficit erased = 1 goal:** 414 comebacks\n- **Deficit erased = 2 goals:** 29 comebacks\n- **Deficit erased = 3+ goals:** **zero**\n\nComebacks happen — but almost never from three down. The biggest deficits\nerased in the dataset were two goals, and even those are rare (~7% of all\ncomebacks).\n\n**Most prolific:** Barcelona, 58 comeback wins in 148 trailed matches\n(39.2%). **Best rate (min. 15 trailed games):** Manchester City WFC at\n45.0%, then PSG 40.0%, Barcelona 39.2%, Arsenal WFC 37.5%. Notably, the\nrate leaders skew toward elite sides — teams good enough to be trailing\nare also good enough to chase.\n\n*Note: StatsBomb data includes women's and youth competitions — \"WFC\"/\"LFC\"\nsuffixes are the women's teams.*\n\n**Limitations.** The analysis is limited to the competitions present in the database; player-level metrics exist only for StatsBomb-covered tournaments, and underlying event data may contain errors.\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "9e4e80b2-5552-47eb-b13d-ba31121f2d15",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Conclusion\n\nIn the StatsBomb subset, 57% of team-matches saw a side trail at some point — and 10.5% of those ended in a comeback win (443 wins). Together these results answer the research question posed in the Introduction and quantify the pattern across 4,200 matches. The notebook provides a fully reproducible pipeline from raw CSV extracts to published figures.\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    }
  ]
}
